A||||31||batboy||V8 conversion problems||||||
Z||000000||batboy||06-13-99||12:51 PM||||Hi, I'm new to the tech forum, but think it's the greatest. I've had my 87 SE (2.5 l) for several years as a daily driver and have been lucky to have only a few minor problems. Tired of having riceburners scream by me and laughing at me, I just bought a 85 2M8 (V8 conversion...hehehe). It was very reasonably priced but needs a bit of work (hence being cheap to buy). Things like bad tie rod ends, a loose wheel bearing, and a clogged rear brake hose wasn't too much of a problem for an experienced amateur shade-tree mechanic such as myself. But, the speedometer is stuck at 20 mph (the previous owner says it does works occasionally for brief periods of times) and the tach is reading incorrectly (orginally a V6 car). I read and reread the discussions here in the forum about speedo and tach problems, but being a geologist, not an electrical engineer, I'm totally clueless. Tach capacitors and filters and printed circuit boards, I'm lost. Same with the speed sensor, what and where the heck is that? Any info source or advice in plain english in layman's terms would be greatly appreciated. I do have a Haynes manual, but not much help there either. Is there any source for an aftermarket V6 to V8 gauges kit? Where can a guy get those 120 mph speedos I keep hearing about? I was going to slap some VDO gauges in, but after reading some of the other discussions, figured out that it's not that easy. Remember, I've never had a Fiero dash apart, how difficult is that? I would prefer not going to digital gauges (I guess I'm old fashioned). Sorry I got so long-winded. Thanks in advance for any help.
Z||000001||Eric||06-13-99||01:31 PM||||I don't know much about electronics either, but I do know about pulling the interior apart.  It's not easy.  Getting behind the dash will take you a few hours.  I'm in the middle of doing an interior restoration and had to take the dash out to paint it.  Luckily, I don't have a problem with my tach or speedo., but I do have a problem with my oil pressure gauge, so I'm just going to replace it with a mechanical one.
Z||000002||theogre||06-13-99||02:48 PM||||The speed sensor is in/on the transmision. sound like whoever did your conversion screwed it up.<p>contact v8archie he can likely help. www.v8archie.com
Z||000003||Larry C||06-13-99||04:23 PM||||I have just taken a 86 GT dash apart to fix the temp gauge for the third time and the first time takes 2-3 hours.  The manual list removing & installing these screws in the following sequence;<br>(1) disconnect the battery<br>(2) remove the lower instrument cluster panel cover<br>(3) two small "star socket" bolts that hold the top cover down, closest to the windshield<br>(4) two bolts that come from the bottom front into the dash, you will see them after you remove the lower dust cover  They are vertical.<br>(5) three "star bolts" across the top, closest to the steering wheel<br>(6) two bolts that are horizontal & at the bottom of the unit<br>(7) two "star bolts" that go into the metal brace in the dash<br>(8) two lower bolts at the front bottom of the cluster<br>(9) there are two bolts that hold the cluster unit into the cover, they are horizontal and at the top corners of the unit.<p>A picture IS worth a thousand words!<br>Best description in the service manual.<br>Larry Clark
Z||000004||batboy||06-13-99||07:35 PM||||Thanks for the suggestions and hints guys. I can probably handle taking the dash apart with some time and patience (and possibly a swear word or two), especially with Larry's detailed description and a manual in front of me. Ogre, I've looked at V8 Archie's web site before and thought about contacting him, but wasn't sure if he could help. At your urging I will now do that. Eric, if I were you, I'd think twice about installing a mechanical oil pressure gauge. I've had two different cars (not Fieros) with aftermarket gauges that the oil line ruptured. These lines are under pressure and spray oil out very quickly. Not only do you run the risk of ruining an engine, I'd hate to think of all that hot oil squirting all over the inside of your car.<p>Ok, I'm going to pursue checking out the speed sensor thing, hopefully that will solve that problem. I got to have a speedometer, I hate getting speeding tickets. I might be able to live with the tach being off for the time being, especially if could figure out approximately how far off it is. Will the 6 cylinder tach be reading higher or lower with a 8 cylinder engine and by how much? If I take the dash apart to replace that tach capacitor like I read about, I'd like to also install a 120 mph speedo. Anyone have a suggestion where I can get one of those puppies. Would V8 Archie's have those too?
Z||000005||Yellow85||06-13-99||07:52 PM||||batboy, between 84-85 doesn't come with 120 mph speedo, but you can find them in 86-88(maybe not all models). I got mine from junk yeard.  By the way, did you receive my email?
Z||000006||Eric||06-13-99||07:59 PM||||Batboy,<br>Do you really think I could have a problem with a mech. gauge?  I know there are risks, but you would know imediatly if something went wrong.  The problem with my current gauge is that it works fine for awhile and then quits or shows really low pressure.  Maybe I have an oil pump problem.  Doesn't the car stop running if there is not enough oil pressure?
Z||000007||Eric||06-13-99||08:01 PM||||I mean stop running because the computer told it to.  Not by blowing up.  Okay, that's better.
Z||000008||batboy||06-13-99||08:10 PM||||Yellow85, I just checked and yes I did get your email, thanks. I guess I thought the 120 speedos were an aftermarket item. I'll try scrounging around, I know a salvage yard with a couple Fieros in it, although they were already kind of picked over. Eric, I've had problems with those aftermarket mechanical gauges before. I have a streetrod (36 Ford truck) right now with a set of VDO electrical gauges in it, no problems in the 4 years I had them installed. By the way, the fuel pump is suppose to cut out when you lose oil pressure in a Fiero, but you still may damage your engine or at least break down and cause a mess. Your choice, but I'll never use a gauge with a oil line again, except possibly using that new hi-fi braided steel lines.
Z||000009||batboy||06-13-99||08:22 PM||||Eric, two other things. Assuming everything worked properly and the fuel pump did shut down when you lost oil pressure, the engine may still run for several seconds due to fuel being in the lines and injectors/carburator. Maybe long enough to cause damage, maybe not. The other advice is, absolutely do not use those nylon/plastic oil pressure lines. They are notorious about splitting and rupturing. Remember, engines are extremely hot and shake and vibrate. If those nylon lines don't melt, they eventually get hard with age and crack from all that movement. This old fart has been there and done that.
Z||000010||Eric||06-13-99||08:34 PM||||Thanks, Batboy.  I don't want to screw with the gauges if I don't have to.  I think the best thing to do would be to work with the existing gauge and find the problem.  Like I said, I don't like the electrics too much, but it's a problem I have to get over.
Z||000011||batboy||06-13-99||08:56 PM||||Eric, I may be stating the obvious, but have you checked out whether or not the oil pressure sending unit is the problem. It would seem to me that this would be cheaper and easier to replace or check then messing with the gauge. I'd start there first if I were you, but you probably already thought of that, I hope.
Z||000012||Eric||06-14-99||07:13 AM||||Yes.  I put a new one in and it didn't do anything.
Z||000013||PaUL||06-14-99||02:09 PM||||Hey batboy, you should replace ALL your gauges with aftermarket ones like I did. I use an electrically driven VDO speedo with the stock speed sensor. Since you probably are not even using the ECM there should be no problem. All the rest of gauges can be replaced with conventional aftermarket units. 
Z||000014||theogre||06-14-99||02:15 PM||||make sure the block is properly grounded to the body. that will also throw off resistor controlled gauges.
Z||000015||batboy||06-14-99||09:24 PM||||PaUL, I'm glad to hear that the electric VDO speedo will work with the speed sensor. That gives me another option. I guess my first step is to make sure the speed sensor is actually working properly then. Yes, my V8 uses a carburetor without any computer crap (yea).<p>Ogre, after reading your post, I looked in the engine compartment and found two body to engine grounding wires disconnected (hmmmm). If I have time tomorrow, I will get some new heavy duty braided grounding straps and see if that makes a difference. Thanks for the heads up.<p>Today the speedo started working for about 4 miles and then quit again!?! I've also determined that the factory water temperature gauge does not work when the key is on, but seems to work when the key is off, strange. I guess that's why they stuck an aftermarket temp gauge into one of the A/C vents. Wires are spliced on this car everywhere...it's a nightmare. But it sure runs good, quick and fast. Did I mention I got this car cheap? Guess I gotta figure on a little blood, sweat, and tears to get everything back into order. It'll be worth it (I hope).
Z||000016||batboy||06-15-99||07:42 PM||||Ogre, I fixed one of those frame to engine grounds and added another heavy duty strap, but no joy, nothing changed. When you said block, you did mean engine block, right? I did find a melted wire (don't know yet where it goes or what it does) laying against the back exhaust manifold. I'll try to work on it some more tomorrow.
Z||000017||theogre||06-16-99||01:12 AM||||to give you an idea...... my 87 4 cyl block has like 6-7 grounded wires on it. Any of them could screw up a number of things, including gauges. checking just the main, obvious, ones may not be enough.
Z||000018||lowCG||06-16-99||03:36 PM||||By properly grounded you mean to the frame,and not just the subframe.Thought I'd mention it,just in case.<br>I solved a lot of problems by running a separate ground cable from the front mounted battery back to the block.<br>Not an electrical thing,but have you had any problems with the front end getting a little "floaty" at speed? Was wondering if a duct,starting at the front of the car,in the airdam,maybe 3"x12" or so,and continuing all the way back to the engine compartment would help.Would make for a cold air supply for the intake as well.
Z||000019||batboy||06-16-99||04:34 PM||||Yes, my V8 Fiero does seem to get very light in the front end at speed above 80 mph or so! But, it does not have a front air dam on it at all. Installing a good front air dam is a major priority for me. I'm also going to relocate the battery from the rear luggage compartment up to the front under the spare tire. Long term plans include swapping the stock cast iron heads for high performance aluminum heads. This is suppose to reduce engine weight by close to 45 pounds, plus significantly increase breathing and power. However, for now I'd be happy just to get all my electrical problems sorted out. It's been raining today, so I haven't been able to work on my batmobile, maybe tomorrow.
Z||000020||PaUL||06-16-99||06:51 PM||||Hey batboy, just out of curiosity, has the decklid on your V8 Fiero been modified? If not, is there enough clearance between the carb and decklid for it to close? Im asking because Im running a carb on mine and want to increase the space to the decklid. Its real tight right now and I have to use a tiny 6 in. aircleaner that gets clogged quickly.
Z||000021||batboy||06-16-99||07:16 PM||||PaUL, no modifications have been done to the rear deck lid. The air cleaner that was on it when I bought it (and still is on it) is one of those super low profile triangular Edelbrock cleaners. I've been thinking of engineering a air cleaner system similar to the factory V6 setup so I could take advantage of cool air ducted in from the outside rather than drawing hot air from inside the engine compartment. Of course, a cooler air/fuel mixture is worth at least a few additional ponies. Do you have a V8 too or is yours a V6?
Z||000022||batboy||06-16-99||07:20 PM||||Oh yeah, PaUL, I forgot to mention...yes I can close the deck lid, just not a lot of extra room as you mentioned. The advantage to that Edelbrock air cleaner is that it flows extremely well and the filter element can be cleaned and reused.
Z||000023||PaUL||06-17-99||01:49 AM||||No badboy, I dont have a V8. I have a 2.8 v6 with a holley 4 bbl carb. I assume your running a similar carb so maybe you can tell me something, what is the total height the air cleaner adds to the top of the carb? I mean to the center wingnut hole.
Z||000024||batboy||06-17-99||08:27 AM||||PaUL, The air cleaner that I'm using on my Edelbrock Performer carb, which should be the same size neck as on your Holley, is the Edelbrock Pro-Flo air cleaner (not the Signature Series triangular one). The filter element "cleans easily with soap and water" according to the ad I'm reading. It costs about $16.00 with replacement elements running about $11.00 through JEGS high performance mail order,  www.jegs.com  I've been extremely pleased with the stuff I've ordered from Jegs and they are really deliver it fast too. Total height is about 2 inches, but since it sits down aways down on the neck of the carb it only sticks up above the carb maybe a 1/2 inch or so. There is no center wing nut sticking out of the top. A special hold down bracket mounts the cleaner from underneath. This is the ultimate low profile air cleaner and will often fit where nothing else will when you have clearance problems.
Z||000025||spike||06-17-99||11:22 AM||||BatBoy, I have been considering a V8 swap in a 87 GT. I have heard the swaping to a V8 makes the car handle like crap. Is this true. I would appreciate an honest answer. I know that it is fast but handling is also very important. Thanks.
Z||000026||batboy||06-17-99||08:24 PM||||Spike, I have two Fieros, one is a 1987 2.5 liter that I've had for about 3 years and the other, of course, is the V8 that I just recently bought. Handling does suffer a little. It is somewhat tail heavy in my opinion. Also, if I'm cruising along and really punch it hard, I can feel it torque a bit sideways. I would not hit a curve quite as hard in the V8 car as I might in my other one. At high speed, the front end feels too light.<p>On the other hand, it handles much better than I expected. (I grew up in the muscle car era and my old Chevelle was a pig when it came to handling.) In easy normal driving mode, you'd barely even realize that you have a V8 back there. I noticed today that all my control arm (A-frame) bushings look bad. Replacing those and adding a rear sway bar has got to help. I have no front air dam, I need to get one. My battery is all the way in the back luggage compartment, so I'm going to move it up front. Eventually I hope to put aluminum heads on my batmobile. That will shave off another 44 pounds or so. After doing the above mentioned modifications, I'm hoping the handling will be greatly improved. I'll keep you guys posted. There, I tried to be as honest as possible. Feel free to ask any other specific questions, I love talking about Fieros.
Z||000027||lowCG||06-17-99||11:36 PM||||I'm going to be talking to an associate soon who used to construct Countach replicas ,about some other business, so I'll try and remember to ask if/what they did to help this, back when they did this sort of thing with the V8 fiero chassis.
Z||000028||spike||06-18-99||11:36 AM||||Thanks for your reply. I also enjoy talking Fieros. Would you happen to know which type of kit was used to install the V8 engine in your car (archie or zumalt). I have an 87 GT that I picked-up for a good price. The body needs new paint but the interior is clean. The big question that still remains to be answered is what engine to install (v8 or v6). The only good thing about a V6 swap is that it is easier and the engine weighs less than a V8. On the other hand the V8 would produce better HP numbers with less mods. I guess there are was to make this type of engine lighter such as al. heads which you mentioned. Well I don't plan on having the car finished this year, but hope by the end of summer I can find enough info to at least decide on what engine to use.<br>Do you happen to have any HP numbers for the engine you have and have you taken it to the track to so what 1/4 mile times you can get.  
Z||000029||batboy||06-18-99||07:09 PM||||A lot of people apparently are dreaming about a V8 Fiero. This idea is very high on the macho scale, but very low on the practical scale. I don't want to B.S. you guys, a V8 Fiero is an expensive, temperamental toy. It compromises handling and reliability for power. It impresses the hell out of most car guys, but most women could care less about what's under the hood, as long as the car looks sharp. The total cash needed to perform the swap properly is probably in the neighborhood of $3,000. Add about another $2,000 for a freshly rebuilt high performance engine. Suddenly, buying a mint 1988 GT V6 and forgetting about this swap crosses your mind. Once the swap is done, it's a bitch to work on in that cramped engine compartment. Changing the spark plugs facing toward the front is a pain. My car, before I bought it, had already destroyed a transmission. You have to cut a hole in the left inner fenderwell to get it to fit. Are you still interested?<p>I don't know who made the V8 kit that's in this car. It looks like it might be a V8 Archie's. I have not taken it to the track yet. The reason I got it so cheap was the previous owner never could get all the problems, mainly electrical, sorted out. The engine is a low mileage 350 out of a truck and is stock other than an Edelbrock carb and a free flowing exhaust system. Plugging the data into my desk-top dyno computer program, puts the output close to 200 horsepower and 300 ft/lbs of torque. The modifications I want to do will bring it up to about 350 ponies and torque to about 400 ft/lbs.<p>Trust me, with that kind of power, I'm going to break stuff on the car every so often. I have several vehicles, more money than brains, and no wife or kids, so I can get away with this project. Again, it's a toy. I'm not trying to scare people away from the swap, just trying to give you good folks a reality check. Undoubtedly, swapping a bigger, hotrod V6 would be more practical, easier, and cheaper, plus the handling would be much better. If you're still determined to go for the V8, then good luck. They are a hoot to drive and will put a grin on your face. :)
Z||000030||batboy||06-18-99||07:17 PM||||Oops, I made a mistake, I meant to say you have to cut a hole in the RIGHT side of the engine compartment, not the left side (the water pump belt actually runs behind the rear tire inside the fenderwell, which is not an ideal location).
Z||000031||lowCG||06-18-99||07:41 PM||||My buddy who,wisely,gave up fiberglass fumes for his own mortgage business has told me that he'd only used '88 models,always installed urethane bushings,and always used Porsche transaxles.Bilsteins were also mandatory.Never had any quirks with his Countach copies.<br>So,I'm thinking that the extra 7" of wheelbase wouldn't hurt the handling any,and would make the package less of a V8 Vega like nightmare.<br>Would look pretty neat too,maybe let the transaxle be seen from the rear,polished,some box flares with an intake scoop in front.<br>The more I look at the '88 front suspension,the more I realize that spring rates play a large part of the improved design,geometry just isn't as much a factor,and even that can be improved to help it to work better in this lightly loaded application. Much bigger brakes would be needed for any serious use also.<br>Guess if I had to have a V8,that'd be the route I'd take.
